data.content:
In .NET 10 (starting with .NET 9 SR10+), `WindowSoftInputModeAdjust.Resize` was deprecated on Android because the underlying `SOFT_INPUT_ADJUST_RESIZE` flag was removed by Google.

The recommended replacement is to use **edge-to-edge display** with `KeyboardInsets`. Here's the approach:

**1. Enable edge-to-edge in your `MainActivity`:**

```csharp
protected override void OnCreate(Bundle? savedInstanceState)
{
    base.OnCreate(savedInstanceState);
    AndroidX.Core.View.WindowCompat.SetDecorFitsSystemWindows(Window, false);
}
```

**2. Use `Padding` bound to keyboard insets in your layout:**

In your page XAML, wrap your content in a `Grid` and set the bottom padding to react to the keyboard:

```xml
<ContentPage xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml">
    <Grid x:Name="RootGrid">
        <!-- Chat messages -->
        <CollectionView Grid.Row="0" ... />

        <!-- Input bar at bottom -->
        <HorizontalStackLayout Grid.Row="1">
            <Entry Placeholder="Type a message..." HorizontalOptions="FillAndExpand"/>
            <Button Text="Send"/>
        </HorizontalStackLayout>
    </Grid>
</ContentPage>
```

**3. Listen for insets in your page code-behind (Android only):**

```csharp
#if ANDROID
using AndroidX.Core.View;
using Microsoft.Maui.Platform;

// In your page constructor or OnAppearing:
var activity = Platform.CurrentActivity;
var rootView = activity?.Window?.DecorView?.RootView;

if (rootView != null)
{
    ViewCompat.SetOnApplyWindowInsetsListener(
        (Android.Views.View)rootView, 
        new KeyboardInsetsListener(insets =>
        {
            RootGrid.Padding = new Thickness(0, 0, 0, insets.Bottom / DeviceDisplay.MainDisplayInfo.Density);
        }));
}
#endif
```

With a helper class:

```csharp
#if ANDROID
class KeyboardInsetsListener : Java.Lang.Object, IOnApplyWindowInsetsListener
{
    private readonly Action<AndroidX.Core.Graphics.Insets> _callback;

    public KeyboardInsetsListener(Action<AndroidX.Core.Graphics.Insets> callback)
        => _callback = callback;

    public WindowInsetsCompat OnApplyWindowInsets(Android.Views.View v, WindowInsetsCompat insets)
    {
        var imeInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.Ime());
        _callback(imeInsets);
        return insets;
    }
}
#endif
```

**Key points:**
- This uses Android's `WindowInsetsCompat.Type.Ime()` which is the modern replacement for `ADJUST_RESIZE`.
- On iOS, MAUI already scrolls content automatically for keyboard avoidance — no extra work needed.
- If you want a simpler (but less flexible) workaround, you can also set `android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustPan"` in your `AndroidManifest.xml`, which scrolls the whole screen up rather than resizing.
